Chapter: Build Settings

 Compiler-wide build parameters. All recursive-descent phases
 reference these values instead of per-file constants.

 LOAD-BEARING: values used in emit functions (code-buffer-size,
 data-buffer-size, accum-capacity, offset-table-size, offset-table-mask)
 are baked into the seed as x86-64 immediates. Changing them requires
 a seed rebuild — the old seed silently uses old values otherwise.

Section: Emit Buffers
  code-buffer-size : Integer = 8388608
  data-buffer-size : Integer = 2097152
  accum-capacity : Integer = 65536

Section: Work Budgets
  max-emit-work : Integer = 16777216
  max-parse-fuel : Integer = 16777216

 max-parse-fuel is a budget for the WHOLE unit, not a bound on recursion,
 and the difference is what BACKLOG 2.26 died of. A parse arm that returns
 without consuming a token spins, and each turn costs a few stack frames
 but only one fuel: the stack runs out a gigabyte down, millions of frames
 deep, long before sixteen million fuel does, so the guard cannot fire and
 the compile double-faults with no diagnostic. Lowering the budget is not
 the answer -- it is sized for the unit's real work, and a budget below
 that cascades into a heap blow-up instead, because an exhausted parse
 call returns an error node and the parse carries on.

 max-parse-stall bounds the thing that actually goes wrong: consecutive
 fuel-consuming calls at the SAME token position. A parse that progresses
 resets it, so no legitimate program can reach it however deeply nested --
 the grammar chain at any one token is tens of calls, not thousands. A
 parse that cannot progress reaches it in microseconds and says so.

  max-parse-stall : Integer = 4096

Section: Demand Decks

 Every phase deck is a generous fixed floor. Address space is the only
 cost - the boot demand-pages the heap range and physical memory
 commits on touch - so the floors are sized for the arena, not for the
 source. These replaced the survey multiplier system, which sized decks
 from source length and corrupted silently when it under-reserved.

 A floor that is not generous ENOUGH fails the same way the multiplier
 did: the parse keep-deck copy (copy-sx-document) writes past the floor
 into the scratch it is still reading, the copier dereferences its own
 overwrite, and the compile dies in a #GP with no diagnostic -- the
 post-copy overflow check never runs. A 444 KB single-unit source (the
 GopBoot boot bundle) was the first to push the copied CST past 64 MB.
 The keep floor now matches the scratch floor's class; the failure
 signature, should any floor under-reserve again, is a silent halt in
 copy-sx-* with a garbage pointer in the fault dump.

 The PARSE floors were not generous, whatever the comment above claimed.
 On 2026-07-13 the compiler grew by about 80 KB of source - the foreword
 chapters behind the quotation gate, chiefly Ed25519 and Sha256 - and
 PARSE overflowed at 192 MB. The floor had been sitting within roughly a
 kilobyte of its limit: the build before the last one passed, and adding
 a single function to a prose block tipped it over. Nobody had noticed
 because nothing had grown the compiler in a while.

 That is the failure this section warns about, arriving exactly as
 predicted, and it is the reason these two are now 384 MB rather than
 192. A floor you can reach by writing one more function is not a floor.

  demand-lex-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-parse-keep-floor : Integer = 402653184
  demand-parse-scratch-floor : Integer = 402653184
  demand-desugar-floor : Integer = 67108864
  demand-scope-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-check-floor : Integer = 671088640
  demand-check-keep-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-frontend-keep-floor : Integer = 201326592
  demand-lower-floor : Integer = 335544320
  demand-resolve-floor : Integer = 201326592
  demand-lift-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-inline-floor : Integer = 100663296

 The quotation gate runs before the frontend does, and it parses the
 source to find the quotations it must gate. That parse needs decks of
 its own, because the frontend has not raised any yet. The scratch floor
 carries the tokens and the syntax tree of a parse that is thrown away,
 so it is sized as a LEX and a PARSE together; the keep floor carries
 the one thing that survives, the text of the works we admitted, so it
 is sized as source text and is checked against before it is written.

 A source that quotes nothing reserves neither.

  demand-quote-keep-floor : Integer = 67108864
  demand-quote-scratch-floor : Integer = 503316480

Section: Recursion Limits
  max-recursion-depth : Integer = 1024

Section: Diagnostics
  max-errors : Integer = 20

Section: Type Checker Pre-Allocation
  substitution-capacity : Integer = 16384
  expr-type-capacity : Integer = 32768

Section: Hash Tables
  offset-table-size : Integer = 16384
  offset-table-mask : Integer = 16383

